course: dictionary
lesson: 4526
title: Headset and the words around it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A headset is a pair of headphones with a small microphone attached, so you can listen and speak at the same time. You can also use a headset for virtual reality, where it sits on your head and shows you a different place. Look at the words around it to be sure which one the writer means.
explain: The word is made of two short words you already know: head and set. A headset is something set on your head. «Elsie wants a £200 gaming headset.» Here the words gaming and wants tell you it is for playing games, so it means headphones with a microphone.
explain: «A VR (virtual reality) headset makes the brain feel somewhere else; the experience is real, but your body stays in the room.» The brackets tell you VR stands for virtual reality. Here the headset is the device for seeing another place, and the words your body stays in the room show that you are still at home.
fact: A headset is a device worn on the head.
fact: A headset usually has headphones and a small microphone, so you can listen and speak.
fact: A VR headset is worn on the head to show virtual reality, a made-up world you can see and move around in.
fact: Keep the volume low on a headset to protect your hearing, and take breaks from a VR headset.
fact: Pilots, call-centre workers and coaches also use headsets to talk and listen.
exercise: «Elsie wants a £200 gaming headset.» What is a headset here? | A hat for the sun | A set of cups for tea | *Headphones with a small microphone, for playing games | A pair of shoes
exercise: In «A VR (virtual reality) headset makes the brain feel somewhere else», what does VR stand for? | Video recording | Voice and radio | Very rapid | *Virtual reality
exercise: Why should you keep the volume low on a headset? | To make the game harder | To make the microphone louder | *To protect your hearing | To make the battery last longer
test: Which job can use a headset to talk and listen? | Painting a fence | Mowing the lawn | *Call-centre worker | Baking bread
test: What does a VR headset make the brain feel? | Cold | *Like it is somewhere else | Hungry | Sleepy in bed
exam: Why is the word headset easy to work out? | It sounds like a kind of food | It is a very old word from a castle | *Its two parts, head and set, are words you already know | It is only used in Scotland
